    ],
    "resultsInterpreter": [
      {
        "display": "Michael Tanaka, MD"
      }
    ],
    "conclusion": "1. 4 mm obstructing calculus at the right ureterovesical junction with mild right hydroureteronephrosis.\n2. 2 mm nonobstructing left lower pole renal calculus.\n3. 22 mm left adrenal nodule measuring 28 HU, indeterminate on this noncontrast exam. Recommend adrenal protocol CT or chemical shift MRI, and consider biochemical evaluation given history of hypertension.\n4. Hepatic steatosis.",
    "presentedForm": [
      {
        "contentType": "text/plain",
        "text": "EXAM:\nCT ABDOMEN AND PELVIS WITHOUT CONTRAST (RENAL STONE PROTOCOL)\n\nCLINICAL INDICATION:\nAcute right flank pain radiating to the groin, with hematuria. History of hypertension. Evaluate for urolithiasis.\n\nTECHNIQUE:\nHelical CT of the abdomen and pelvis was performed without intravenous or oral contrast using a low-dose renal stone protocol. Coronal and sagittal reformats were reviewed. CTDIvol: 8.4 mGy. DLP: 402 mGy-cm.\n\nCOMPARISON:\nNone available.\n\nFINDINGS:\nLower chest: Lung bases are clear. Heart size is normal.\nLiver and biliary: Diffusely decreased hepatic attenuation consistent with steatosis. No focal lesion identified on this noncontrast exam. Gallbladder is unremarkable. No biliary dilatation.\nSpleen and pancreas: Unremarkable.\nAdrenal glands: 22 mm well-circumscribed left adrenal nodule with attenuation of 28 HU (series 2, image 41). The right adrenal gland is normal.\nKidneys, ureters, and bladder: 4 mm calculus at the right ureterovesical junction with mild right hydroureteronephrosis and mild perinephric stranding. 2 mm nonobstructing calculus in the lower pole of the left kidney. No left hydronephrosis. The bladder is partially distended and otherwise unremarkable.\nBowel and appendix: Normal appendix. No bowel obstruction or bowel wall thickening.\nLymph nodes and vessels: No lymphadenopathy. Mild atherosclerotic calcification of the abdominal aorta, which is normal in caliber.\nPeritoneum: No free fluid or free air.\nBones and soft tissues: No aggressive osseous lesion.\n\nIMPRESSION:\n1. 4 mm obstructing calculus at the right ureterovesical junction with mild right hydroureteronephrosis.\n2. 2 mm nonobstructing left lower pole renal calculus.\n3. 22 mm left adrenal nodule measuring 28 HU, indeterminate on this noncontrast exam. Recommend adrenal protocol CT or chemical shift MRI, and consider biochemical evaluation given history of hypertension.\n4. Hepatic steatosis."
      }
    ]
  },
